      <h3>Stage 1: Entity Clarity</h3>

      <p>Entity clarity is the foundation of AI search visibility. AI models do not think in keywords — they think in entities. An entity is a distinct, identifiable thing: a business, a person, a place, a concept. Your goal is to make your business an unambiguous entity that AI systems can identify and reference with confidence.</p>

      <p>For a Birmingham business, entity clarity requires:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>NAP consistency</strong> — your Name, Address, and Phone number must be identical across your website, Google Business Profile, Companies House listing, Yell.com, and every directory or platform where you appear. Inconsistencies create entity confusion for AI systems.</li>
        <li><strong>Google Knowledge Panel</strong> — claiming and optimising your Knowledge Panel establishes your business as a recognised entity in Google's Knowledge Graph, which feeds into AI Overviews.</li>
        <li><strong>Wikidata and Wikipedia</strong> — for larger Birmingham businesses, a Wikidata entry creates a persistent entity reference that AI training data picks up. This is the gold standard for entity recognition.</li>
        <li><strong>Clear "About" page</strong> — your <a href="/about/">about page</a> should contain a definitive, structured description of your business that reads like an encyclopedia entry: who you are, what you do, where you operate, when you were established, and why you are authoritative in your field.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Stage 2: Structured Data</h3>

      <p>Structured data provides the machine-readable schema that AI systems use to verify and categorise your business. While traditional SEO uses structured data for rich results, AI search uses it as a factual backbone.</p>

      <p>Essential schema types for AI search optimisation include:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>LocalBusiness or Organization</strong> — establishes your business entity with name, address, contact details, and service area.</li>
        <li><strong>Service</strong> — defines each service you offer, linking it to your business entity and geographic coverage.</li>
        <li><strong>FAQPage</strong> — marks up questions and answers so AI systems can extract them directly. FAQ schema is particularly powerful for AI citation because it pre-formats content in the exact question-answer structure AI models use.</li>
        <li><strong>HowTo</strong> — structured step-by-step instructions that AI systems can present as procedural answers.</li>
        <li><strong>Product</strong> — for Birmingham retailers, product schema with pricing, availability, and reviews provides the structured data AI shopping assistants need.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Stage 3: Content Authority</h3>

      <p>Content authority for AI search is different from content authority in traditional SEO. AI systems do not reward word count or keyword frequency. They reward comprehensiveness, directness, and verifiability.</p>

      <p>Authoritative content for AI search follows these principles:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Open every section with a declarative statement that could be quoted verbatim by an AI system.</li>
        <li>Include specific, verifiable data points rather than vague claims. "Birmingham has over 700 jewellery businesses in the Jewellery Quarter" is citable. "Birmingham has a thriving jewellery scene" is not.</li>
        <li>Structure content with clear heading hierarchies that signal topic relationships.</li>
        <li>Answer the most common questions about your topic directly, without requiring the reader (or AI system) to infer the answer from surrounding text.</li>
        <li>Update content regularly with visible timestamps. AI systems prioritise fresh sources.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Stage 4: Citation Signals</h3>

      <p>Citation signals are the AI-era equivalent of backlinks. When your business is mentioned consistently across authoritative third-party sources, AI systems treat those mentions as validation of your entity's authority and accuracy.</p>

      <p>Effective citation signals for Birmingham businesses include:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Mentions on industry bodies (The Law Society, ICAEW, RICS, relevant trade associations)</li>
        <li>Features in local and national media (Birmingham Mail, Birmingham Live, BBC Midlands)</li>
        <li>Consistent directory listings (Yell, Bark, Clutch, Trustpilot, Google Business Profile)</li>
        <li>Guest content on authoritative sites in your sector</li>
        <li>Client testimonials and reviews that mention your business name and location</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Stage 5: AI Visibility</h3>

      <p>The final stage ensures AI systems can physically access and process your content. This involves technical implementation that many Birmingham businesses overlook:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>llms.txt file at your domain root (detailed below)</li>
        <li>robots.txt configured to allow AI crawlers</li>
        <li>Content formatted for direct extraction (quick answer boxes, tables, numbered lists)</li>
        <li>Regular monitoring of AI search platforms to track when and how your business is cited</li>
      </ul>
